592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087875 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Initial Value Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087876 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1045 "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Polynomial Forcing" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087946 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f8700d0a-d600-5dce-b743-5464f739c710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088228 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 8y =-16$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a601b5b-b013-5c42-bdd5-a25b3a11ff4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "870e7b8e-206b-50c2-8d89-7f75a9431965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088363 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "513cfc62-eb9a-585a-a155-a58861456868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088364 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "445996cc-ad9d-514f-8bb7-d053c9192103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088365 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddb29a9d-408f-5d53-82cb-356f77e1a234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088381 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088450 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y =-12$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62a4e5ba-fa3d-5d0d-9956-72e1733faeec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088811 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088811 #edb/ref 1061 594357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088811 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4969b0a4-b67d-5988-bc46-595ff3f801e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088811 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088812 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088812 #edb/ref 1061 594358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088812 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "002aa299-2816-508f-a9df-3570e71ccec6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088812 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088813 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088813 #edb/ref 1061 594359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088813 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79ad3266-720c-5773-97c4-728bb8e1d170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088813 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088814 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088814 #edb/ref 1061 594362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088814 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d09ba57-93a7-5c6d-819f-04fafb053441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088814 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088815 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088815 #edb/ref 1061 594365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088815 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72b183f7-614e-5652-8a6d-5612612ec685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088815 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088816 #edb/ref 1061 594368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088816 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a18ed00-78ec-5ad9-aaca-85ae16bd0bd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088816 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088946 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a second-order homogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only.\n\nA **second-order inhomogeneous** differential equation is similar, except the right-hand side does *not* equal zero. So, a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation can be written as\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nFor example, the following equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE because the right-hand side is $3$ instead of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3\n$$\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **polynomial forcing** if $f(x)$ is a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088946 #edb/ref 1065 4061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088946 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e9adc7d-336d-5b0f-96ca-a19f64b5714f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088946 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088953 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the general solution to an inhomogeneous ODE, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the solution $y_c$ of the associated homogeneous ODE. This solution is called the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**).\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p$ (also known as the **particular integral**) of the inhomogeneous ODE. If $f(x)$ is an $n$th degree polynomial, then so is a particular solution.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: $y = y_c + y_p.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the general solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda=-3$ or $\\lambda=-2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-2x}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right-hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+5(0)+6\\alpha =3\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088953 #edb/ref 1065 4136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088953 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "632695f2-f752-5080-b0e9-8a624d205221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088953 #edb/ref 1067 "Solving Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342517 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21a85e00-002e-526f-aa68-9fa9a9e3cd49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342760 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f517e31-a1d3-5caa-8710-7ee592d9e883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342957 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342957 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342957 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "565ce293-b429-5b97-aecf-cf9c7e935987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343698 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343698 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343698 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5f3d97f-80c2-5792-afc6-911099c236aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344207 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344207 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344207 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e06fa21a-8c01-5a09-8cc4-778680199c15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344726 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "84373811-cac1-5c50-acaa-26cce938f12f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a92cf087-3d29-5414-a809-a999e198051b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345325 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345360 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345360 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345360 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded875ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345383 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03917c72-354d-53bd-8eb1-b175d01190d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345469 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345469 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345469 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e6f8c3a-e267-5331-9844-155baa68208c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345590 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ba85172-106c-541a-a67c-8d14ff82d144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345991 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345991 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345991 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ab841b6-2b4a-5ee2-b66b-fcf171e89172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346055 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346055 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346055 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "51c26de1-64db-53ff-a3b5-4cb2aa7cbbf5" #edb/re